---
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';
import { REPO } from '../lib/releases.js';

const CONTACT = 'qrate@dvnl.work';
---

<Legal
  title="Terms of service"
  eyebrow="Terms"
  updated="25 November 2026"
  description="Terms for qrate and this website: AGPL-3.0 free software, no warranty, pre-release builds, your catalogue stays yours, and the optional Google and AI services."
  lede="qrate is free software under AGPL-3.0. These terms cover this website and the optional services the application can connect to."
>
  <h2>The software</h2>
  <p>
    qrate is licensed under the <a href="/license">GNU Affero General Public License v3.0</a>.
    That license, not this page, governs your right to run, study, modify and share the software. If
    the two ever disagree, the license wins. Running qrate to catalogue a collection creates no
    obligation to anyone, at any scale.
  </p>

  <h2>No warranty</h2>
  <p>
    qrate is provided <strong>as is</strong>, without warranty of any kind, express or implied,
    including merchantability and fitness for a particular purpose. To the extent the law allows, no
    contributor is liable for any damages arising from its use, including loss of data. This is the
    plain-language version of sections 15 and 16 of the AGPL, which are the operative text.
  </p>

  <h2>Pre-release builds</h2>
  <p>
    Beta and alpha builds, which you can choose under <strong>Settings ▸ Updates</strong>, are
    unfinished. They can contain defects that damage or lose a project file. Whatever build you use, <strong>keep backups of anything you care about</strong>, and treat qrate as one tool in your
    workflow rather than the only copy of your collection.
  </p>

  <h2>Your data is yours</h2>
  <p>
    A project is a file on your own disk. We have no access to it, claim no ownership or license over
    your catalogue or your linked files, and operate no service that stores them. You are responsible
    for having the right to catalogue the material you put into it, and for meeting any rules your
    own institution places on that material.
  </p>

  <h2>Connected services</h2>
  <p>
    qrate can connect to services you choose: Google Drive and Sheets for export and sync, an AI
    model provider for the optional assistant, and whatever a plugin you install points at. Those
    connections are between you and that provider, under their terms. For Google, those are the <a href="https://policies.google.com/terms" rel="noopener">Google Terms of Service</a>; for the
    assistant's default provider, the <a href="https://openrouter.ai/terms" rel="noopener"
      >OpenRouter Terms of Service</a
    > and the terms of the model provider that handles each request. You are responsible for having
    permission to use the accounts you connect, and for having the right to send your collection
    data to them. We do not sit in the middle of those connections and cannot restore data lost
    inside them. A Google Sheets sync replaces the contents of the sheet you choose. See the <a
      href="/privacy">privacy policy</a
    > for exactly what is sent and when.
  </p>

  <h2>The AI assistant</h2>
  <p>
    The assistant's suggestions and changes come from an AI model and can be wrong, incomplete or
    invented. Review what it proposes before you accept it, and check changes it makes in edit mode;
    each one is recorded in History and can be undone. You remain responsible for your catalogue and
    for what you publish from it.
  </p>

  <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
  <p>
    Do not use qrate or its connected integrations to break the law, to infringe someone else's
    rights, or in a way that violates a connected provider's terms. Do not represent a modified build
    as an official one. The AGPL requires a modified version to carry prominent notice of the change.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    The site is provided as is, and may be offline or out of date at any time. The build reads the
    release information from GitHub, so it can lag the repository. GitHub serves the downloads
    themselves, under their terms. The builds are unsigned, so your operating system may warn you on
    first launch. If that matters to you, check a download against the published checksums.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    These terms may change. The date at the top of this page changes with them, and earlier versions
    stay in the site's <a href={`https://github.com/${REPO}`} rel="noopener">git history</a>.
    Continuing to use qrate after a change means you accept the current terms.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions, or commercial licensing: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>. Bugs are best
    filed as <a href={`https://github.com/${REPO}/issues`} rel="noopener">issues</a>, with steps that
    reproduce them.
  </p>
</Legal>
